1. What is the Collection Framework?
The Java Collection is a Framework used for storing, manipulating, and processing groups of objects.

It provides:
Interfaces
Implementations (classes)
Algorithms

2. Why Collections Are Needed
Dynamic size (unlike arrays)
Built-in methods for searching, sorting, filtering
Type safety with Generics
Performance-optimized data structures
Widely used in enterprise & Spring Boot applications

3. Collection Hierarchy (High Level)
Iterable
 └── Collection
      ├── List
      │     ├── ArrayList
      │     ├── LinkedList
      │     └── Vector
      ├── Set
      │     ├── HashSet
      │     ├── LinkedHashSet
      │     └── TreeSet
      └── Queue
            ├── PriorityQueue
            └── ArrayDeque
⚠️ Map is not part of Collection interface but part of Collection Framework.

 List vs Set vs Map:
| Feature    | List  | Set | Map     |
| ---------- | ----- | --- | ------- |
| Order      | Yes   | No  | No      |
| Duplicates | Yes   | No  | Keys No |
| Access     | Index | No  | Key     |


4. List Interface
Allows duplicate elements
Maintains insertion order
Index-based access

ArrayList:
Dynamic array
Fast read, slow insert/delete (middle)

List<String> list = new ArrayList<>();
list.add("Java");
list.add("Spring");
list.add("Java");

System.out.println(list);

Output
[Java, Spring, Java]

LinkedList:
  Doubly linked list
  Fast insert/delete, slow access
  
  List<Integer> list = new LinkedList<>();
  list.add(10);
  list.add(20);
  
  System.out.println(list.get(1));
  
  Output
  20

ArrayList vs LinkedList (Interview Favorite)
| Feature        | ArrayList     | LinkedList         |
| -------------- | ------------- | ------------------ |
| Data Structure | Dynamic Array | Doubly Linked List |
| Access         | Fast          | Slow               |
| Insert/Delete  | Slow          | Fast               |
| Memory         | Less          | More               |

5. Set Interface
 No duplicates
 No index-based access

HashSet:
 Uses Hashing
 No order guarantee

 Set<Integer> set = new HashSet<>();
 set.add(10);
 set.add(20);
 set.add(10);
 
 System.out.println(set);
 Output
 csharp
 Copy code
 [10, 20]

LinkedHashSet:
Maintains insertion order

 Set<String> set = new LinkedHashSet<>();
 set.add("A");
 set.add("B");
 set.add("A");
 
 System.out.println(set);
 
 Output
 [A, B]

TreeSet:
Sorted order
Uses Red-Black Tree

Set<Integer> set = new TreeSet<>();
set.add(30);
set.add(10);
set.add(20);

System.out.println(set);

Output
[10, 20, 30]

HashSet vs TreeSet
| Feature      | HashSet | TreeSet      |
| ------------ | ------- | ------------ |
| Order        | No      | Sorted       |
| Performance  | Faster  | Slower       |
| Null allowed | Yes     | No (Java 8+) |

6. Queue Interface
 Follows FIFO
 PriorityQueue
 Elements processed by priority
 
 Queue<Integer> q = new PriorityQueue<>();
 q.add(30);
 q.add(10);
 q.add(20);
 
 System.out.println(q.poll());
 
 Output
 10

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
8. Map Interface
Stores key-value pairs

HashMap:
No order
Allows one null key

Map<Integer, String> map = new HashMap<>();
map.put(1, "Java");
map.put(2, "Spring");

System.out.println(map.get(1));

Output:
Java

LinkedHashMap:
Maintains insertion order

TreeMap:
Sorted by key

Map<Integer, String> map = new TreeMap<>();
map.put(3, "C");
map.put(1, "A");
map.put(2, "B");

System.out.println(map);

Output
{1=A, 2=B, 3=C}

HashMap vs Hashtable
| Feature     | HashMap | Hashtable   |
| ----------- | ------- | ----------- |
| Thread-safe | No      | Yes         |
| Null key    | Allowed | Not allowed |
| Performance | Faster  | Slower      |
| Legacy      | No      | Yes         |

9. Iteration Techniques
Using for-each
for(String s : list) {
    System.out.println(s);
}

Using Iterator
Iterator<String> it = list.iterator();
while(it.hasNext()) {
    System.out.println(it.next());
}


✔ Allows safe removal

10. Generics with Collections

Ensures type safety

List<String> list = new ArrayList<>();
// list.add(10); ❌ Compile-time error

11. Sorting Collections
Using Collections.sort()
List<Integer> list = Arrays.asList(3,1,2);
Collections.sort(list);

System.out.println(list);

Output
[1, 2, 3]

Custom Sorting (Comparator)
Collections.sort(list, (a,b) -> b - a);

12. Fail-Fast vs Fail-Safe
Fail-Fast
Throws ConcurrentModificationException

Example: ArrayList
Fail-Safe
Works on copy

Example: CopyOnWriteArrayList

13. Concurrent Collections (Important for 4+ yrs)
| Collection           | Use Case          |
| -------------------- | ----------------- |
| ConcurrentHashMap    | High concurrency  |
| CopyOnWriteArrayList | Read-heavy apps   |
| BlockingQueue        | Producer-Consumer |

14. Collections vs Arrays
| Arrays             | Collections     |
| ------------------ | --------------- |
| Fixed size         | Dynamic         |
| Faster             | Slightly slower |
| No utility methods | Rich APIs       |

